                <note rend="inline" type="source">893.51/5326: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Minister in China</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">Johnson</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to
                        the Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Peiping</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1930-07-10T16:00:00+08:00">July 10, 1930—4
                        p.m.</date><lb/>[Received July 10—1:34 p.m.]</dateline></opener>
                <p>560. Legation’s 551, July 8, 11 a.m. Following from American Consul General at
                    Shanghai: <quote rend="blockquote">
                        <p>“July 8, 5 p.m. The steamship <hi rendition="#italic">Golden Horn</hi> of
                            the States Steamship Company paid tonnage dues and received certificate
                            of payment at Tientsin on June 30th. Upon entering at Shanghai the
                            customs demanded a new payment. This office made representation to the
                            Commissioner of Customs that the payment having been made at Tientsin
                            the vessel was entitled to the use of the ports without other tonnage
                            dues for a period of four months. Upon the receipt of a letter from this
                            Consulate General claiming exemption from further payment of tonnage
                            dues, they issued a new certificate without additional payment taking up
                            the one issued at Tientsin. This is a valuable precedent which may be
                            useful at other ports.”</p>
                    </quote></p>
                <closer>For the Minister:<lb/><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                        >Perkins</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
